I think it’s better to start with a simple example of a compiler optimization. Let’s take a simple example of a loop that can be optimized.
Here’s a simple code:
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;
    int z = x + y;
}
The compiler can optimize this loop in several ways. One of the most common optimizations is called constant folding. In this case, the variables x and y are constants, so their values can be computed at compile-time. So, the code can be optimized to:
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    int z = 10 + 20;
}
Another optimization is called loop invariant code motion. In this case, the code inside the loop (the assignment of x, y, and z) is not dependent on the loop variable i. So, the compiler can move this code outside the loop:
int x = 10;
int y = 20;
int z = x + y;
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    // do nothing
}
This optimization can significantly reduce the number of operations performed inside the loop, which can improve the performance of the program.
Another optimization is called dead code elimination. In this case, the variable z is assigned but never used. So, the compiler can eliminate the assignment of z:
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;
}
This optimization can reduce the number of operations and the amount of memory used.
These are just a few examples of compiler optimizations. There are many more, and each compiler may implement different optimizations depending on the target platform and the language being compiled.
